From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Eric Reinsmidt <735251+ericreinsmidt@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] hciattach_xradio: a checksum that survives GCC 15

check_sum16 read the packet back through an unsigned short pointer right
after the packet's struct fields were written, which C's aliasing rules
allow the compiler to reorder: built with GCC 15 at -O2, every firmware
block went out with a stale checksum, the chip answered each with
MB_ERR_CHECKSUM (resp error flag, type 3), never started the firmware, and
never answered HCI Reset. Summed a byte pair at a time instead: the same
little-endian halfwords, no aliasing. Tested on the TrimUI Brick: no
checksum errors, the firmware starts, hci0 comes up.
---
--- a/tools/hciattach_xradio.c
+++ b/tools/hciattach_xradio.c
@@ -400,15 +400,19 @@
 static unsigned short check_sum16(unsigned char *data, unsigned int len)
 {
     unsigned short cs = 0;
-    unsigned short *p = (unsigned short*)data;
 
-    while(len > 1) {
-        cs += *p++;
+    /* The halfwords a byte pair at a time, little-endian as the chip sums
+     * them, not through an unsigned short pointer: the buffers are filled
+     * through their structs' wider fields, and reading them back as shorts
+     * breaks C's aliasing rules, so GCC 15 summed them before the writes
+     * landed and the chip refused every firmware block (checksum error) */
+    while (len > 1) {
+        cs += data[0] | (data[1] << 8);
+        data += 2;
         len -= 2;
     }
-    if (len) {
-        cs += *(unsigned char*)p;
-    }
+    if (len)
+        cs += *data;
 
     return cs;
 }
